WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.418
In this lesson we load a small table of currency exchange rates from a

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.580
web page, and format it as a table visual.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.407
With Get data from the Web, Power BI reads the page, lists the tables

4
00:00:21.407 --> 00:00:24.497
it finds, and loads the one we choose.

5
00:00:24.497 --> 00:00:27.200
While it loads, the window says Evaluating.

6
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.790
The new table appears in the Data pane.

7
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:52.674
I add a Table visual to the canvas and tick the currency column and

8
00:00:52.674 --> 00:00:53.720
the rate column.

9
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.344
Now the formatting.

10
00:01:07.344 --> 00:01:14.515
Select the visual, open the Format pane with the paintbrush icon, and work through the sections:

11
00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:18.100
style presets, grid, values, column headers, and totals.

12
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.180
Under Values, I increase the font size and choose a clear font, so the

13
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.030
numbers are easy to read.

14
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.360
I also make the visual larger on the canvas.

15
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.229
It is good practice to check the numbers against the source, so I compare

16
00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:07.740
the rates with the website.

17
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.604
Back in Power BI, I sort the table by clicking a column header.

18
00:02:24.604 --> 00:02:28.500
Now the currencies with the highest rates are at the top.

19
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.813
Small touches like a readable font, a sorted order, and clear headers make even

20
00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:46.720
a simple table much easier to understand.

